{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"graphic-novel-track","kind":"workflow","title":"Graphic novel track","description":"Takes a graphic novel from idea to a production-ready first chapter in gated steps (concept and page budget, chapters, page breakdown, chapter one script, production plan).","category":"comics","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["plan","design","build"],"role":["writer","artist"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["text","notes"],"output":["plan","outline","script","table"],"risk":"read-only","invocation":"user","effort":"deep","interaction":"interactive","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"intermediate","depth":"basic","tags":["graphic-novel-development","page-budget","chapter-breakdown","full-script","production-plan"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["review-comic-script","thumbnail-comic-page","plan-comic-production-schedule","write-graphic-novel-pitch"],"personas":["comics-editor"]},"args":[{"name":"idea","description":"The premise, main characters, genre, audience, how the story ends if you know, and any length or format you have in mind.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"team_and_time","description":"Who will draw, colour and letter, how many hours a week each can give, and any deadline (a pitch, a crowdfunding campaign, a contest). Optional.","type":"text"}],"steps":[{"id":"concept","file":"steps/01-concept.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"graphic-novel/01-concept.md"},{"id":"chapters","file":"steps/02-chapters.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"graphic-novel/02-chapter-breakdown.md"},{"id":"pages","file":"steps/03-pages.md","stage":"design","gate":"approve","artifact":"graphic-novel/03-page-breakdown.md"},{"id":"script","file":"steps/04-script.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve","artifact":"graphic-novel/04-chapter-one-script.md"},{"id":"production","file":"steps/05-production.md","stage":"plan","gate":"none","artifact":"graphic-novel/05-production-plan.md"}],"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"Develops a graphic novel the way a comics editor would: decide the length and shape before scripting, budget pages before writing panels, and prove the approach on one chapter before committing to the whole book. Each step builds on the one before and stops for the creator's approval.\n\n<idea>\n{{idea}}\n</idea>\n{{#team_and_time}}\n<team_and_time>\n{{team_and_time}}\n</team_and_time>\n{{/team_and_time}}\n\nRules for every step: the story belongs to the creator, so offer options and explain trade-offs rather than replacing their choices; never contradict an approved step without flagging it; keep page arithmetic consistent across steps; one moment per panel and roughly 200 to 250 lettered words per page at most; never invent publishers, comparable titles or platform rules. If the creator wants to jump straight to scripting, explain in one line why the page budget comes first, offer to run steps 1 and 2 quickly, and keep every gate.","steps":[{"id":"concept","file":"steps/01-concept.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"graphic-novel/01-concept.md","text":"# Step 1: Concept and page budget\n\nSettle what the book is before planning any pages.\n\n1. If the idea lacks a protagonist, a central conflict or an audience, ask up to three questions and stop.\n2. Write the logline in one sentence and the story spine in three: the beginning, the turn, the ending (if the ending is unknown, offer two options and mark it as a decision).\n3. Set the audience age category and content limits, and the format: a single volume or a series of volumes, colour or black and white, print or digital first.\n4. Propose a total page count that fits the audience and the story's scope, and explain the trade-off (longer books cost more time and money to make). For print, keep the total a multiple of four including front matter.\n5. Check the page count against the team and time if given: pages per week times weeks available. If it does not fit, say so and offer a shorter version.\n\nWrite the document with sections Logline and spine, Audience and format, Page count, Fit with team and time, Decisions.\n\nStop and wait for approval of the page count and format before breaking the story into chapters."},{"id":"chapters","file":"steps/02-chapters.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","artifact":"graphic-novel/02-chapter-breakdown.md","text":"# Step 2: Chapter breakdown\n\nDivide the approved story into chapters that each work as a unit.\n\n1. Break the story into chapters (or volumes, then chapters) with a page budget for each that adds up to the approved total.\n2. For each chapter give what happens, the main character's state at the start and end, the key image or set piece, and the hook or turn that leads into the next chapter.\n3. Mark the story's major beats (inciting incident, midpoint, crisis, climax) by page number and check that the climax gets enough pages.\n4. Flag chapters that carry too much for their page budget, and subplots that never pay off.\n\nWrite the document with sections Chapter table (Chapter | Pages | What happens | Key image | Hook), Story beats by page, Flags.\n\nStop and wait for approval before breaking chapter one into pages."},{"id":"pages","file":"steps/03-pages.md","stage":"design","gate":"approve","artifact":"graphic-novel/03-page-breakdown.md","text":"# Step 3: Page breakdown for chapter one\n\nPlan chapter one page by page before writing panels.\n\n1. Ask whether page 1 of the book is a right-hand page (standard for print) and state the assumption; for digital-first, plan reveals by screen or scroll instead.\n2. For every page of chapter one give: the page number and side, what happens, the panel count (fewer for big moments, more for fast exchanges), and whether it ends on a pull to turn the page.\n3. Place the chapter's reveals at the top of left-hand pages, after a turn.\n4. Note splash pages and spreads, and the pages that are time-heavy to draw (crowds, vehicles, complex locations) so the artist sees them early.\n\nWrite the document with sections Assumptions, Page table (Page | Side | Panels | What happens | Turn or hook), Heavy pages.\n\nStop and wait for approval before scripting."},{"id":"script","file":"steps/04-script.md","stage":"build","gate":"approve","artifact":"graphic-novel/04-chapter-one-script.md","text":"# Step 4: Script chapter one\n\nWrite chapter one in full-script form from the approved page breakdown.\n\n1. Use a PAGE heading with number, side and panel count, PANEL headings with a present-tense description for the artist (shot size, angle, who is where, action, expression, key props), and numbered lettering beneath (CAPTION, CHARACTER, SFX).\n2. Keep one moment per panel, and keep dialogue to the lines that move the scene; let the art carry what it can show.\n3. After the script, count panels and lettered words per page and flag any page over the budget.\n4. Add notes for the artist (recurring motifs, key acting moments, character consistency) and list any invented visual details as questions.\n\nWrite the document with sections Script, Load counts, Notes for the artist, Questions.\n\nStop and wait for approval or revisions before the production plan."},{"id":"production","file":"steps/05-production.md","stage":"plan","gate":"none","artifact":"graphic-novel/05-production-plan.md","text":"# Step 5: Production plan\n\nTurn the approved chapter into a plan the team can start on.\n\n1. List the production stages for this book (thumbnails, pencils, inks, flats, colours, letters, production pass) and who owns each.\n2. Ask each person to time two or three pages of chapter one, and give a provisional per-page estimate marked as a guess until then.\n3. Estimate how long chapter one and the whole book will take at the stated hours, with a buffer of about 15 to 20 percent, and say whether any deadline is realistic.\n4. Say what to make next for the creator's goal: for a pitch, which finished sample pages and design sheets; for a webcomic or crowdfunding launch, how many finished pages to bank first.\n5. List the next five actions in order, each with an owner.\n\nWrite the document with sections Stages and owners, Time estimate, Deadline check, What to make next, Next actions.\n\nThis is the last step. Offer to repeat steps 3 and 4 for the next chapter."}]}
